course: adult-life
lesson: 34
title: When grown-ups are tired or worried
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Grown-ups get tired and worried too, even when they look as if they are coping with everything. Work, housework, money, sick relatives and bad news all use up energy and patience, and it shows in their voice and their face.
explain: Scene: in Melbourne, Dad drops his bag by the door: «I've had a long day.» He is quiet and grumpy until tea, then he tells jokes. By eight o'clock Mum is asleep on the sofa with the telly still on. Nothing terrible happened; they were just worn out.
explain: Tiredness and worry are usually not about you. A grumpy hello at the door means the work day is still hanging on, and «I need five minutes» means a grown-up is calming down instead of snapping.
explain: Some worry is quieter: a tight, careful voice on the phone to the bank, a phone checked again and again, red eyes and «I'm fine, honestly». You don't have to fix it. A few quiet minutes, a hug, or a simple «Are you OK?» often help most.
fact: «I've had a long day» means a grown-up's energy and patience are used up, and a rest and food usually bring them back.
fact: Work can be tiring in the head, not only in the body.
fact: Tired adults often push on all day and only stop when they sit down, and a good night's sleep usually fixes it.
fact: «I need five minutes» is a grown-up's way of calming down instead of snapping, and afterwards they are usually kinder.
fact: A work mood can follow a grown-up home and usually fades within an hour or so.
fact: A deadline is the time by which work must be finished; stress is the tight, worried feeling of too much to do in too little time.
fact: Money worries often show in the voice; most families have tight months, and fixing them is not a child's job.
fact: A worried mind keeps going back to the same thing, so worried adults can be distracted until the news comes.
fact: «I'm fine» often means «I'm not fine, but I don't want to talk about it now».
fact: Adults sometimes switch off heavy news to take a break from it, because too much bad news makes anyone gloomy.
fact: A grown-up's tiredness or bad mood is usually not about the child and not a sign they are cross with them.
exercise: Dad drops his bag: «I've had a long day.» What does he mean? | His energy and patience are used up | He worked for exactly 24 hours | He is cross with you about something | He wants to go out again at once
exercise: Mum falls asleep on the sofa at eight with the telly on. What is going on? | She is worn out from her day | She is ill and needs an ambulance | She is pretending to avoid you | She has decided to sleep there forever
exercise: Mum holds up a hand: «I need five minutes.» What should you do? | Wait, then come back later | Keep talking until she listens | Go and ask Dad the same thing | Walk off and never ask again
exercise: Dad comes home grumpy but is cheerful after dinner. Why? | His work mood faded with food and home | He was cross with you until dinner | He was only pretending to be grumpy | Dinner had medicine in it to cheer him
exercise: Mum at the laptop late: «I'm so stressed about this deadline.» What is a deadline? | The time by which work must be finished | A line on the floor nobody may cross | The day a job ends forever | A rule that stops you working late into the night
exercise: Dad's voice goes tight and careful on the phone to the bank. What might it be about? | A bill or a month where money is short | A prize he has just won | Booking a table at a restaurant for a birthday | Ordering a new football for you
exercise: Is it your job to fix your family's money problems? | No, that's for the grown-ups | Yes, you should give all your savings | Yes, you should get a job after school | No, because money problems fix themselves
exercise: Mum keeps checking her phone and isn't really listening. What could you ask? | «Are you waiting for something?» | «Why are you ignoring me again?» | «Can I have your phone now?» | «Is your phone broken or what?»
exercise: Auntie, with red eyes and a wobbly smile: «I'm fine, honestly.» What does it often mean? | She is sad but doesn't want to talk now | She is completely happy and fine | She has a cold and sore eyes | She wants you to leave the house right now
exercise: What can you do for Auntie when she says she's fine with red eyes? | Sit nearby or give her a simple hug | Make her tell you everything now | Tell everyone she has been crying | Laugh to cheer her up about it
exercise: Grandad sighs at the news on the radio and switches it off. Why? | He's taking a break from heavy news | The radio has run out of batteries | He wants to listen to music instead | He is cross with the newsreader
exercise: Is a grown-up's tiredness usually about you? | No, it's usually about their own day | Yes, it means you did something wrong | Yes, children always make adults tired | No, grown-ups never really get tired
exercise: Mum's asleep with the telly on loud. What could you do? | Turn the volume down or put a blanket on her | Wake her up to ask about tomorrow's trip to the shops | Turn it up so she wakes up | Ring Gran to tell her Mum is ill
exercise: When should you ask Dad a big question after his long day? | After he has had a few quiet minutes | The second he walks in the door | Never, tired dads can't answer | While he is on the phone to the bank
exercise: Why do grown-ups say «I need five minutes» instead of snapping? | It stops a feeling spilling out on someone | It is a rule they learned at work on a course | They want you to count to 300 | They are timing how long you wait
exercise: Mum's deadline stress lasts all week. What helps? | Helping with small jobs and being easy | Asking her lots of questions at night | Telling her to stop working at once | Making a loud mess to cheer her up
test: Your brother says «Dad's grumpy 'cos he hates us.» Put him right. | He's tired from work and it'll pass | Yes, Dad has stopped loving us | Dad is grumpy because he is ill | It's because we didn't tidy up last year
test: Overheard: «Absolutely knackered, love, been on my feet since six.» What does she mean? | She is extremely tired | She hurt her knee at work | She has been asleep since six | She wants to go running now
test: Dad, on the phone: «Yes... I understand... so when will the payment go through?» His voice is tight. Which is most likely? | He's sorting out a money problem | He's ordering a pizza for tea | He's chatting to an old friend from school | He's booking a holiday for fun
test: Sister to Mum: «Muuum, MUM, look, MUM!» Mum: «Give me five minutes or I'll lose it.» What's going on? | Mum needs a short break to stay calm | Mum wants exactly five more toys | Mum is going to lose her phone | Mum will be out for five minutes
test: Gran in Dublin texts Mum all day from hospital. Mum keeps checking her phone and doesn't hear your joke. Why? | She's worried and waiting for news | She doesn't like your jokes any more | She's playing a game on her phone | She's cross because you were loud
test: Little brother: «If Mum says she's fine, she's fine, stop asking.» What's the better idea? | Don't push her, but stay close and kind | Keep asking until she tells you everything | Ignore her completely all day | Tell her she's lying to you
test: What would happen if Dad rushed into a talk about your report card the second he got home from a horrible day? | He might snap, though he wouldn't mean it | He'd be extra patient and cheerful all evening | He'd forget all about work at once | He'd give you double pocket money
test: Mum's friend: «I'm running on fumes this week.» What does she mean? | She's very tired and has little energy left | Her car smells of petrol fumes | She has started running every single morning | She's been cooking a lot of smoky food
test: The night after the deadline, Mum is laughing and relaxed. Why? | The work is handed in, so the stress has gone | She lost her job and doesn't care about anything | She stayed up all night again | The deadline was moved to tomorrow
test: You hear scary news about prices on the radio and Grandad switches it off. What's a good thing to do? | Ask him about it if it worries you | Keep worrying about it on your own | Turn it back on as loud as you can | Tell your friends that you're all poor now
test: Big sister: «Mum's always knackered, she must be really ill.» Is she right? | Not usually; a good night's sleep often fixes it | Yes, being tired always means a bad illness | Yes, grown-ups never get tired for no reason | No, Mum is just pretending to be tired
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression «I have had a long day»? | The speaker's energy and patience have been substantially depleted | The speaker's working day has exceeded twenty-four hours in length | The speaker intends to reprimand the listener | The speaker wishes to commence further work
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the request «I need five minutes». | To regain composure before attending to the matter | To measure the patience of the person waiting | To defer the matter until the following day | To indicate that the request has been refused
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning financial difficulty within a household. | Its resolution is the responsibility of the adults | Its resolution is the duty of the eldest child | It is invariably caused by the children's conduct | It is a rare occurrence affecting few families
exam: It is given that a relative with reddened eyes declares «I'm fine, honestly». Determine the most appropriate response. | Offer quiet company without pressing for an explanation | Insist upon an immediate and full explanation of the distress | Inform the remainder of the household at once | Accept the statement and depart without a word
